# REST Controllers & OpenAPI

Controllers are thin incoming adapters: validate input, call the use case port, map the sealed `Result` to an HTTP response. **Every controller and endpoint carries OpenAPI annotations.**

## Rules

- Include `quarkus-smallrye-openapi` in every service.
- Annotate every resource class with `@Tag` and every endpoint with `@Operation` plus `@APIResponse` entries for each outcome the sealed `Result` can produce:

```java
@Path("/positions")
@Tag(name = "Positions", description = "Portfolio position management")
public class PositionResource {

    @POST
    @Operation(summary = "Create a position", description = "Creates a new portfolio position from a transaction")
    @APIResponse(responseCode = "201", description = "Position created",
            content = @Content(schema = @Schema(implementation = PositionResponse.class)))
    @APIResponse(responseCode = "409", description = "Position already exists")
    @APIResponse(responseCode = "400", description = "Invalid request")
    public Uni<Response> create(@Valid CreatePositionRequest request) {
        return createPositionUseCase.execute(mapper.toCommand(request))
                .onItem().transform(this::toResponse);
    }
}
```

- Document DTO fields with `@Schema(description = ..., example = ...)` on request/response records.
- Map each sealed `Result` variant to a distinct, documented status code; the `@APIResponse` list should mirror the `Result` variants exhaustively.
- Endpoints are reactive: return `Uni<Response>`/`Uni<Dto>`, never block.
- Bean Validation (`@Valid`, constraint annotations) on request DTOs at the boundary; the core re-enforces domain invariants independently.
